多根第二病院","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":34.65518,"lo":135.434999,"d":2094},{"n":"コトブキ薬局","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":34.654735,"lo":135.435762,"d":2133},{"n":"Konohana Sakurajima Post Office","g":"essentials","k":"post_office","la":34.662254,"lo":135.428882,"d":2208,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-17:00","ph":"+81-6-6461-5015"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":34.662293,"lo":135.428929,"d":2214},{"n":"Port of Osaka","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.641944,"lo":135.422778,"d":842,"ds":"The Port of Osaka (大阪港, Ōsaka-kō) is the main port in Japan, located in Osaka within Osaka Bay. The Port of Osaka also has several sister ports including the Port of Busan.","wpu":"Port of Osaka","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Osakaharbornorth-2.jpg/960px-Osakaharbornorth-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Osaka Maritime Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.640047,"lo":135.404572,"d":1161,"ds":"The Osaka Maritime Museum (なにわの海の時空館, Naniwa no Umi no Jikūkan) was a maritime museum in Osaka, Japan. It was opened by the Mayor of Osaka City on 14 July 2000 having started on site in March 1998. Designed by architect Paul Andreu with engineering design by Arup and Tohata.","wpu":"Osaka Maritime Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Osaka_maritime_museum01s3200.jpg/960px-Osaka_maritime_museum01s3200.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Yumemai Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.658833,"lo":135.399583,"d":2006,"ds":"The Yumemai Bridge (夢舞大橋, yumemai ōhashi) is a floating moveable bridge in Konohana District, Osaka, Japan. It spans the North Waterway connecting the man-made islands Yumeshima and Maishima of Osaka Port. It comprises a floating bridge over the waterway, transitional girder bridges on both ends of the floating bridge, and approach bridges on Yumeshima and Maishima.","wpu":"Yumemai Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Yumemai_Ohashi_Osaka_JPN_001%282%29.jpg/960px-Yumemai_Ohashi_Osaka_JPN_001%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Minato Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.644069,"lo":135.437629,"d":2081,"ds":"The Minato Bridge is a double-deck cantilever truss bridge in Osaka, Japan; upper deck is for Hanshin Expressway Route 16 Osakako Line, and lower deck is Route 5 Bayshore Line. It opened in 1974. It is the third-longest cantilever truss span in the world, behind the Quebec Bridge and the Forth Bridge.","wpu":"Minato Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Minato_Bridge_1181902.jpg/960px-Minato_Bridge_1181902.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Konohana Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.6665,"lo":135.414639,"d":2279,"ds":"The Konohana Bridge (此花大橋, Konohana Ō-hashi) is a self-anchored suspension bridge located in Osaka, Japan. Opened for traffic in 1990, it has a main span of 300 metres (980 ft). The bridge's unusual design has only a single main cable.","wpu":"Konohana Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Konohana_Ohashi_Osaka_JPN_001.jpg/960px-Konohana_Ohashi_Osaka_JPN_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Yumeshima","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.652,"lo":135.388,"d":2558,"ds":"Yumeshima (夢洲) is an artificial island in Osaka Bay. It is part of Konohana-ku (此花区), one of the 24 wards of Osaka, Japan. It is near the mouth of the Yodo River.","wpu":"Yumeshima","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/%E5%A4%A2%E6%B4%B2.JPG/960px-%E5%A4%A2%E6%B4%B2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Maishima","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.664222,"lo":135.395139,"d":2721,"ds":"Maishima (舞洲) is an artificial island located in Konohana-ku, Osaka, Osaka Prefecture Japan. The area is about 220 hectares. One of the three artificial island districts that was the subject of the \"Technoport Osaka\" plan formulated in 1988 with the aim of developing a new city center.","wpu":"Maishima","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/%E8%88%9E%E6%B4%B2.JPG/960px-%E8%88%9E%E6%B4%B2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Yahataya Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.6605,"lo":135.4472,"d":3357,"ds":"The Yahataya Sports Park Center (八幡屋スポーツパークセンター, Yahataya Supōtsu Pāku Sentā) also known as Yahataya Park (八幡屋公園, Yahataya kōen) is a park in Osaka, Japan It was established in April 1923 as the city athletic field which hosted the 1923 Far Eastern Championship Games. It later became a venue for international trade fairs.","wpu":"Yahataya Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Yahataya-park.jpg/960px-Yahataya-park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Osaka Bay Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.669327,"lo":135.461151,"d":4954,"ds":"The Osaka Bay Tower (大阪ベイタワー), also known as ORC 1, is a mixed-use, mainly hotel and office, skyscraper in the Osaka Resort City 200 building complex located in the Minato-ku ward of Osaka, Japan. Completed in March 1993, it stands at 200 m (656 ft) tall, with the top floor located at 188.7 m (619 ft). It is the 5th tallest building in Osaka Prefecture and the 36th tallest building in Japan.","wpu":"Osaka Bay Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/The_Bay_Tower_Hotel_-cropped.jpg/960px-The_Bay_Tower_Hotel_-cropped.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Kyoto ( or ; Japanese: 京都, Kyōto [kʲoꜜːto] ), officially Kyoto City (京都市, Kyōto-shi; [kʲoːtoꜜɕi] ), is the capital city of Kyoto Prefecture in the Kansai region of Japan's largest and most populous island of Honshu. As of 2020, the city had a population of 1.46 million, making it the ninth-most populous city in Japan. More than half (56.8%) of Kyoto Prefecture's population resides in the city.","wpu":"Kyoto","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Kyoto%2C_Japan_%2849667780482%29.jpg/960px-Kyoto%2C_Japan_%2849667780482%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}